<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Terracotta Army</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 246–<a href="/years/209-bce">209 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Terracotta Army is a collection of life-sized terracotta figures of soldiers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> and horses buried with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Qin Shi Huang</a>, the first emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, in 210–209 BCE to protect him in the afterlife.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/terracotta-army.jpg" alt="Terracotta Army" width="330" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: xiquinhosilva · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:51714-Terracota-Army.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The standardised currency, weights, measures and writing outlived the dynasty, as did the roads, the northern walls that became the base of the Great Wall of China, and the Mausoleum of Qin Shi Huang with its <b>Terracotta Army</b>, not discovered until 1974.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Qin's material culture is known from the inscribed Stone Drums usually attributed to Qin, the bronze bells of its dukes and the vast tomb complex of the First Emperor near Xi'an, with its <b>terracotta army</b>, begun when Zheng was still king of Qin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
